characteristicPolynomial(Matroid) -- computes characteristic polynomial of a matroid
************************************************************************************

  * Function: *note characteristicPolynomial: (Posets)characteristicPolynomial,
  * Usage: 
        characteristicPolynomial M
  * Inputs:
      * M, a *note matroid: Matroid,, 
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note VariableName: (Posets)characteristicPolynomial, => ..., default
        value q,
  * Outputs:
      * a *note ring element: (Macaulay2Doc)RingElement,, the characteristic
        polynomial of M

Description
===========

The characteristic polynomial is a particular specialization of the Tutte
polynomial. If M is a matroid of rank r with Tutte polynomial T(x, y), then the
characteristic polynomial of M is given by (-1)^r * T(1 - x, 0).

This function computes the characteristic polynomial as an evaluation of the
Tutte polynomial. If the Tutte polynomial of the matroid has already been
computed, then this function should return the characteristic polynomial
instantaneously.



+-----------------------------------------+
|i1 : M = matroid completeGraph 4         |
|                                         |
|o1 = a "matroid" of rank 3 on 6 elements |
|                                         |
|o1 : Matroid                             |
+-----------------------------------------+
|i2 : T = tuttePolynomial M               |
|                                         |
|      3    3     2            2          |
|o2 = x  + y  + 3x  + 4x*y + 3y  + 2x + 2y|
|                                         |
|o2 : ZZ[x, y]                            |
+-----------------------------------------+
|i3 : factor characteristicPolynomial M   |
|                                         |
|o3 = (x - 3)(x - 2)(x - 1)               |
|                                         |
|o3 : Expression of class Product         |
+-----------------------------------------+

Caveat
======

If M = M(G) is a graphic matroid, then the characteristic polynomial of M and
the chromatic polynomial of G differ by a factor of x^k, where k is the number
of connected components of the graph G.

See also
========

  * *note tuttePolynomial(Matroid): tuttePolynomial_lpMatroid_rp, -- Tutte
    polynomial of a matroid
  * *note chromaticPolynomial: chromaticPolynomial, -- computes chromatic
    polynomial of a graph

Ways to use this method:
========================

  * *note characteristicPolynomial(Matroid):
    characteristicPolynomial_lpMatroid_rp, -- computes characteristic
    polynomial of a matroid
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

