chromaticPolynomial -- computes chromatic polynomial of a graph
***************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        chromaticPolynomial G
  * Inputs:
      * G, a *note graph: (Graphs)Graph,, 
  * Outputs:
      * a *note ring element: (Macaulay2Doc)RingElement,, the chromatic
        polynomial of G

Description
===========

The chromatic polynomial is an invariant of a graph that counts the number of
vertex colorings. The value of this polynomial at a natural number n is the
number of ways to color the vertices of G using at most n colors, such that no
adjacent vertices have the same color.

This method computes the chromatic polynomial as a multiple of the
characteristic polynomial of the graphic matroid. Indeed, if M = M(G) is the
graphic matroid corresponding to a graph G, then the chromatic polynomial of G
equals the characteristic polynomial of M times x^k, where k is the number of
connected components of G (which is distinct from the number of *note
components: components_lpMatroid_rp, of M).



+---------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : factor chromaticPolynomial cycleGraph 7             |
|                                                         |
|                        2            2                   |
|o1 = (x)(x - 2)(x - 1)(x  - 3x + 3)(x  - x + 1)          |
|                                                         |
|o1 : Expression of class Product                         |
+---------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : factor characteristicPolynomial matroid cycleGraph 7|
|                                                         |
|                     2            2                      |
|o2 = (x - 2)(x - 1)(x  - 3x + 3)(x  - x + 1)             |
|                                                         |
|o2 : Expression of class Product                         |
+---------------------------------------------------------+



The Four Color Theorem states that if G is a planar graph, then its chromatic
polynomial has value > 0 at n = 4. In accordance with this, we see that K5 is
not planar (on the other hand, note that K_{3,3} is bipartite, hence
2-colorable):



+-----------------------------------------------+
|i3 : factor chromaticPolynomial completeGraph 5|
|                                               |
|o3 = (x)(x - 4)(x - 3)(x - 2)(x - 1)           |
|                                               |
|o3 : Expression of class Product               |
+-----------------------------------------------+

See also
========

  * *note tuttePolynomial(Matroid): tuttePolynomial_lpMatroid_rp, -- Tutte
    polynomial of a matroid
  * *note characteristicPolynomial(Matroid):
    characteristicPolynomial_lpMatroid_rp, -- computes characteristic
    polynomial of a matroid

Ways to use chromaticPolynomial:
================================

