indicesOf -- indices of a sublist
*********************************

  * Usage: 
        indicesOf(E, L)
        indicesOf(M, L)
  * Inputs:
      * E, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, 
      * M, a *note matroid: Matroid,, 
      * L, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, a list of sublists of E, or a
        sublist of M_*
  * Outputs:
      * a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, of indices

Description
===========

This method has two typical-use cases. The first case is to convert sublists of
a list E to their corresponding indices. For technical reasons, the accepted
input is a list L of sublists of E, and the output is a list of sets of
indices, one set for each sublist in L. Note that the order of elements in the
sublist is lost, when viewed as a set.



+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : indicesOf(toList(a..z) | toList(0..9), {{m,a,c,a,u,l,a,y,2},{i,s},{f,u,n|
|                                                                             |
|o1 = {set {0, 2, 20, 24, 11, 28, 12}, set {8, 18}, set {20, 13, 5}}          |
|                                                                             |
|o1 : List                                                                    |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|}})                                                                          |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+



The second case is with a matroid as input. Here the ambient list E is taken as
the ground set of the matroid (i.e. E = M_*), and L should be a list of
elements of M (not a list of lists); in this case the inverse of this method is
given by *note taking subscripts: Matroid _us List, with respect to M.



+-----------------------------------------+
|i2 : M = matroid({a,b,c,d},{{a,b},{a,c}})|
|                                         |
|o2 = a "matroid" of rank 2 on 4 elements |
|                                         |
|o2 : Matroid                             |
+-----------------------------------------+
|i3 : B = {a, c}                          |
|                                         |
|o3 = {a, c}                              |
|                                         |
|o3 : List                                |
+-----------------------------------------+
|i4 : S = indicesOf(M, B)                 |
|                                         |
|o4 = {0, 2}                              |
|                                         |
|o4 : List                                |
+-----------------------------------------+
|i5 : M_S == B                            |
|                                         |
|o5 = true                                |
+-----------------------------------------+



In this case, if L is not a sublist of M_*, then a warning is printed, and L
itself is returned. This is done so that if a user inputs a list of indices,
then it will be interpreted as a set of indices. For more on this, cf. *note
groundSet: groundSet,.

See also
========

  * *note groundSet: groundSet, -- (internal) ground set
  * *note Matroid _ List: Matroid _us List, -- elements of matroid
  * *note relabel: relabel, -- relabel a matroid

Ways to use indicesOf:
======================

  * "indicesOf(List,List)"
  * "indicesOf(Matroid,List)"

For the programmer
==================

The object *note indicesOf: indicesOf, is a *note method function:
(Macaulay2Doc)MethodFunction,.

