matroid -- constructs a matroid
*******************************

  * Usage: 
        M = matroid(E, B)
        M = matroid(E, C, EntryMode => "circuits")
        M = matroid(E, N, r)
        M = matroid(B)
        M = matroid(A)
        M = matroid(G)
        M = matroid(I)
  * Inputs:
      * E, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, a ground set
      * n, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, the size of the ground set
      * B, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, a list of bases
      * C, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, a list of circuits
      * N, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, a list of non-spanning circuits
      * r, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, the target rank
      * A, a *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix,, whose column vectors form the
        ground set
      * G, a *note graph: (Graphs)Graph,, whose edges form the ground set
      * I, an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, a squarefree monomial ideal
        defining the independence complex
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note EntryMode: matroid_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmEntryMode=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => ...,
        default value "bases", select method of specifying matroid
      * Loops => ..., default value {}
      * ParallelEdges => ..., default value {}
  * Outputs:
      * M, a *note matroid: Matroid,, 

Description
===========

The default representation of a matroid in this package is by its ground set
and list of bases.



+-----------------------------------------------+
|i1 : M = matroid({a,b,c,d}, {{a,b},{a,c}})     |
|                                               |
|o1 = a "matroid" of rank 2 on 4 elements       |
|                                               |
|o1 : Matroid                                   |
+-----------------------------------------------+
|i2 : peek M                                    |
|                                               |
|o2 = Matroid{bases => {set {0, 1}, set {0, 2}}}|
|             cache => CacheTable{...1...}      |
|             groundSet => set {0, 1, 2, 3}     |
|             rank => 2                         |
+-----------------------------------------------+



One can create a matroid by specifying its *note circuits: circuits, or *note
nonbases: nonbases, instead, using the option *note EntryMode:
matroid_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmEntryMode=>_pd_pd_pd_rp,. Regardless of the value of
EntryMode, the bases are automatically computed in the process of creation.



+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : M = matroid({a,b,c,d},{}, EntryMode => "nonbases") -- defaults to       |
|                                                                             |
|o3 = a "matroid" of rank 4 on 4 elements                                     |
|                                                                             |
|o3 : Matroid                                                                 |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|uniform matroid of full rank                                                 |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : peek M                                                                  |
|                                                                             |
|o4 = Matroid{bases => {set {0, 1, 2, 3}}  }                                  |
|             cache => CacheTable{...2...}                                    |
|             groundSet => set {0, 1, 2, 3}                                   |
|             rank => 4                                                       |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : N = matroid({a,b,c,d}, {{b,c}}, EntryMode => "circuits")                |
|                                                                             |
|o5 = a "matroid" of rank 3 on 4 elements                                     |
|                                                                             |
|o5 : Matroid                                                                 |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : peek N                                                                  |
|                                                                             |
|o6 = Matroid{bases => {set {0, 2, 3}, set {0, 1, 3}}}                        |
|             cache => CacheTable{...3...}                                    |
|             groundSet => set {0, 1, 2, 3}                                   |
|             rank => 3                                                       |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+



If no ground set is provided, the ground set is taken to be the (sorted) union
of the bases/nonbases/circuits.



+-----------------------------------------------+
|i7 : M = matroid {{a,b},{a,c}}                 |
|                                               |
|o7 = a "matroid" of rank 2 on 3 elements       |
|                                               |
|o7 : Matroid                                   |
+-----------------------------------------------+
|i8 : peek M                                    |
|                                               |
|o8 = Matroid{bases => {set {0, 1}, set {0, 2}}}|
|             cache => CacheTable{...1...}      |
|             groundSet => set {0, 1, 2}        |
|             rank => 2                         |
+-----------------------------------------------+



If an integer n is the first argument, the ground set is taken to be {0, ...,
n-1}.





The non-spanning circuits, together with the target rank, may also be
specified.



+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i9 : matroid(toList(0..<8), {{0,1,2,3},{2,3,4,5},{0,1,6,7},{4,5,6,7}}, 4) == |
|                                                                             |
|o9 = true                                                                    |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|swirl 4                                                                      |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+



If a matrix is provided, then the realizable matroid on the columns of the
matrix is returned. The ground set consists of columns of the matrix, and
independence is determined by the method *note rank: (Macaulay2Doc)rank, (which
allows flexibility over general rings understood by M2).



+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i10 : M = matroid random(ZZ^3, ZZ^5)                                         |
|                                                                             |
|o10 = a "matroid" of rank 3 on 5 elements                                    |
|                                                                             |
|o10 : Matroid                                                                |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i11 : peek M                                                                 |
|                                                                             |
|o11 = Matroid{bases => {set {0, 1, 2}, set {0, 1, 3}, set {0, 2, 3}, set {1, |
|              cache => CacheTable{...3...}                                   |
|              groundSet => set {0, 1, 2, 3, 4}                               |
|              rank => 3                                                      |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|2, 3}, set {4, 0, 1}, set {4, 0, 2}, set {4, 1, 2}, set {4, 0, 3}, set {4, 1,|
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|3}, set {4, 2, 3}}}                                                          |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+



If a graph is provided, then the graphic matroid is returned. The ground set
consists of edges in the graph, and circuits are precisely the (minimal) cycles
in the graph.



+------------------------------------------------------------+
|i12 : M = matroid completeGraph 3                           |
|                                                            |
|o12 = a "matroid" of rank 2 on 3 elements                   |
|                                                            |
|o12 : Matroid                                               |
+------------------------------------------------------------+
|i13 : peek M                                                |
|                                                            |
|o13 = Matroid{bases => {set {1, 2}, set {0, 2}, set {0, 1}}}|
|              cache => CacheTable{...6...}                  |
|              groundSet => set {0, 1, 2}                    |
|              rank => 2                                     |
+------------------------------------------------------------+



One can use the optional arguments Loops and ParallelEdges to specify loops and
parallel edges for the graph, respectively (as the *note Graphs: (Graphs)Top,
package does not currently provide functionality for loops or parallel edges).
These options are intended only for use with graphic matroids. ParallelEdges
should be given as a list of edges (which are two-element sets of the form
set$\{i,j\}$ where i, j are vertices in G), and Loops should be given as a list
of vertices where the loops are based.



+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i14 : M = matroid(completeGraph 3, ParallelEdges =>                          |
|                                                                             |
|o14 = a "matroid" of rank 2 on 8 elements                                    |
|                                                                             |
|o14 : Matroid                                                                |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|{set{0,1},set{0,1},set{1,2}}, Loops => {0,2})                                |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i15 : peek M                                                                 |
|                                                                             |
|o15 = Matroid{bases => {set {4, 5}, set {5, 3}, set {5, 1}, set {0, 5}, set  |
|              cache => CacheTable{...5...}                                   |
|              groundSet => set {0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7}                      |
|              rank => 2                                                      |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|{4, 2}, set {4, 1}, set {2, 3}, set {1, 3}, set {1, 2}, set {0, 2}, set {0,  |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|1}}}                                                                         |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i16 : circuits M                                                             |
|                                                                             |
|o16 = {set {0, 1, 2}, set {1, 2, 3}, set {0, 3}, set {4, 1, 2}, set {4, 3},  |
|      -----------------------------------------------------------------------|
|      set {4, 0}, set {0, 5, 1}, set {5, 1, 3}, set {4, 5, 1}, set {5, 2},   |
|      -----------------------------------------------------------------------|
|      set {6}, set {7}}                                                      |
|                                                                             |
|o16 : List                                                                   |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+



If a squarefree monomial ideal is provided, corresponding to a simplicial
complex $\Delta$ via the Stanley-Reisner correspondence, then the matroid with
*note independence complex: independenceComplex_lpMatroid_rp, $\Delta$ is
returned. The ground set consists of the variables in the ring of the ideal.



+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i17 : R = QQ[x_0..x_4]                                                       |
|                                                                             |
|o17 = R                                                                      |
|                                                                             |
|o17 : PolynomialRing                                                         |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i18 : I = monomialIdeal (x_0*x_1*x_3,x_1*x_2*x_4,x_0*x_2*x_3*x_4)            |
|                                                                             |
|o18 = monomialIdeal (x x x , x x x , x x x x )                               |
|                      0 1 3   1 2 4   0 2 3 4                                |
|                                                                             |
|o18 : MonomialIdeal of R                                                     |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i19 : M = matroid I                                                          |
|                                                                             |
|o19 = a "matroid" of rank 3 on 5 elements                                    |
|                                                                             |
|o19 : Matroid                                                                |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i20 : peek M                                                                 |
|                                                                             |
|o20 = Matroid{bases => {set {4, 2, 3}, set {4, 1, 3}, set {4, 0, 3}, set {4, |
|              cache => CacheTable{...2...}                                   |
|              groundSet => set {0, 1, 2, 3, 4}                               |
|              rank => 3                                                      |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|0, 2}, set {4, 0, 1}, set {1, 2, 3}, set {0, 2, 3}, set {0, 1, 2}}}          |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

Caveat
======

This function does not check if (E,B) defines a matroid - see *note
isWellDefined: isWellDefined_lpMatroid_rp,.

The bases are not stored as sets of elements of M - rather, the indices (with
respect to the ground set) are stored instead. For more, see *note groundSet:
groundSet,.

See also
========

  * *note isWellDefined(Matroid): isWellDefined_lpMatroid_rp, -- whether the
    input is a well-defined matroid
  * *note bases: bases, -- bases of matroid
  * *note indicesOf: indicesOf, -- indices of a sublist
  * *note specificMatroid: specificMatroid, -- creates built-in matroid

Ways to use matroid:
====================

  * "matroid(Graph)"
  * "matroid(Ideal)"
  * "matroid(List)"
  * "matroid(List,List)"
  * "matroid(List,List,ZZ)"
  * "matroid(List,MonomialIdeal)"
  * "matroid(Matrix)"
  * "matroid(ZZ,List)"

For the programmer
==================

The object *note matroid: matroid, is a *note method function with options:
(Macaulay2Doc)MethodFunctionWithOptions,.

