Matroid ++ Matroid -- direct sum of matroids
********************************************

  * Operator: *note ++: (Macaulay2Doc)++,
  * Usage: 
        M ++ N
  * Inputs:
      * M, a *note matroid: Matroid,, 
      * N, a *note matroid: Matroid,, 
  * Outputs:
      * a *note matroid: Matroid,, the direct sum of M and N

Description
===========

The direct sum of M and N is a matroid with ground set equal to the disjoint
union of the ground sets of M and N, and bases equal to the union of bases of M
and N.



+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : S = uniformMatroid(2,3) ++ uniformMatroid(1,3)                          |
|                                                                             |
|o1 = a "matroid" of rank 3 on 6 elements                                     |
|                                                                             |
|o1 : Matroid                                                                 |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : peek S                                                                  |
|                                                                             |
|o2 = Matroid{bases => {set {0, 1, 3}, set {4, 0, 1}, set {0, 5, 1}, set {0,  |
|             cache => CacheTable{...1...}                                    |
|             groundSet => set {0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5}                             |
|             rank => 3                                                       |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|2, 3}, set {4, 0, 2}, set {0, 5, 2}, set {1, 2, 3}, set {4, 1, 2}, set {5, 1,|
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|2}}}                                                                         |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : S_*                                                                     |
|                                                                             |
|o3 = {(0, 0), (1, 0), (2, 0), (0, 1), (1, 1), (2, 1)}                        |
|                                                                             |
|o3 : List                                                                    |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : (S ++ uniformMatroid(1, 3))_*                                           |
|                                                                             |
|o4 = {((0, 0), 0), ((1, 0), 0), ((2, 0), 0), ((0, 1), 0), ((1, 1), 0), ((2,  |
|     ------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|     1), 0), (0, 1), (1, 1), (2, 1)}                                         |
|                                                                             |
|o4 : List                                                                    |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

Caveat
======

The elements of the ground set of the direct sum will receive placeholders to
ensure disjointness (as evidenced by the elements of S being ordered pairs
above). As this method is binary, repeated applications of this function will
result in nested placeholders. Since the bases are stored as indices, the bases
of M will not change, but those of N will be shifted up by the size of the
ground set of M.

See also
========

  * *note Matroid + Matroid: Matroid + Matroid, -- union of matroids
  * *note components(Matroid): components_lpMatroid_rp, -- connected components
    of matroid

Ways to use this method:
========================

  * *note Matroid ++ Matroid: Matroid ++ Matroid, -- direct sum of matroids
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

