sum2 -- 2-sum of matroids
*************************

  * Usage: 
        sum2(M, N)
  * Inputs:
      * M, a *note matroid: Matroid,, 
      * N, a *note matroid: Matroid,, 
  * Outputs:
      * a *note matroid: Matroid,, the 2-sum of M and N with basepoint 0

Description
===========

This function returns the 2-sum of two given matroids M and N (cf. Oxley,
Section 7.1).

It is always assumed that the common basepoint of M and N is the first element
in the respective ground sets, i.e. the element with index 0. (To form a 2-sum
using a different basepoint, one can first *note relabel: relabel, M and/or N.)
Moreover, it is necessary that the basepoint 0 is not a *note loop: loops, or
*note coloop: coloops, in either M or N. Under these assumptions, the 2-sum of
M and N is equal to the *note contraction: contraction, of the *note
seriesConnection: seriesConnection, of M and N by 0 (or alternatively, the
deletion of the *note parallelConnection: parallelConnection, of M and N by 0).

The operation of 2-sum is important in higher matroid connectivity: a *note
connected: isConnected_lpMatroid_rp, matroid is *note 3-connected:
is3Connected, iff it cannot be expressed as a 2-sum of smaller matroids.



+----------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : M = sum2(specificMatroid "fano", uniformMatroid(2,4))|
|                                                          |
|o1 = a "matroid" of rank 4 on 9 elements                  |
|                                                          |
|o1 : Matroid                                              |
+----------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : isConnected M                                        |
|                                                          |
|o2 = true                                                 |
+----------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : is3Connected M                                       |
|                                                          |
|o3 = false                                                |
+----------------------------------------------------------+

See also
========

  * *note seriesConnection: seriesConnection, -- series connection of two
    matroids
  * *note is3Connected: is3Connected, -- whether a matroid is 3-connected
  * *note getSeparation: getSeparation, -- finds a k-separation of a matroid
  * *note isConnected(Matroid): isConnected_lpMatroid_rp, -- whether a matroid
    is connected

Ways to use sum2:
=================

  * "sum2(Matroid,Matroid)"

For the programmer
==================

